WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.680
A familiar question rocks the Marvel Cinematic
Universe: "What If?"

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:09.360
In episode one of the new animated series,
the question gets applied to Agent Carter.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.620
Specifically: What If Peggy Carter stayed
in the room when Steve Rogers became Captain

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:14.620
America?

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:20.460
"Wouldn't you be more comfortable in the booth?"

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.769
"No, I'd prefer to stay."

00:00:24.769 --> 00:00:26.439
The answer, it seems, is chaos.

00:00:26.439 --> 00:00:30.189
During Hydra's attack on the Super Soldier
program by undercover members of the State

00:00:30.189 --> 00:00:35.110
Department, Peggy's slightly altered proximity
to the super-soldier serum changes the whole

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:36.110
game.

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:40.300
Steve is shot before he can receive the life-altering
injections and lies bleeding on the floor,

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:44.460
while the capable Carter drops the saboteur
and tells Steve to stay put.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:49.280
Short on time and bereft of other options,
Carter climbs into the transformation chamber

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.550
and gets pumped full of the serum herself.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:55.769
She emerges transformed, a towering goliath
of proper British strength.

00:00:55.769 --> 00:01:00.320
She receives a shield all her own adorned
with the Union Jack, and almost immediately

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:04.850
starts beating her alternate universe American
counterpart at the "winning World War II"

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:09.020
game, nabbing the Tesseract from Arnim Zola
without much muss or fuss.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.340
Steve rehabilitates and, with the help of
Howard Stark's engineering prowess, starts

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:18.770
piloting a suit of Hydra Stomper armor, powered
by the Allies' recently acquired Tesseract.

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:23.079
Newly outfitted in a plate-armored mech suit,
powered by an Infinity Stone and an insatiable

00:01:23.079 --> 00:01:27.821
patriotic go-get-em attitude, Steve Rogers
enters the fray and joins Captain Carter on

00:01:27.821 --> 00:01:28.821
the battlefield.

00:01:28.821 --> 00:01:32.350
"Let's hear it for Captain Carter."

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:36.829
But the fascist-smashing good times can't
last forever, and Captain Carter finds herself

00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:40.729
at her lowest point right around the end of
the story's second act.

00:01:40.729 --> 00:01:44.700
During "Operation: Where Eagles Dare," Steve
is apparently killed in action thanks to a

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.510
stash of dynamite hidden on a Hydra train.

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:51.670
Peggy interrogates an imprisoned Arnim Zola,
learning that the Red Skull is planning to

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:55.960
bust out an interdimensional champion at Castle
Krake in the Black Forest.

00:01:55.960 --> 00:02:00.909
Accompanied by the Howling Commandos, Howard
Stark, and Bucky Barnes, Cap invades the castle.

00:02:01.909 --> 00:02:05.960
Inside of Castle Krake, our heroes discover
that the Red Skull has captured Rogers, the

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:08.920
Hydra Stomper armor, and the Tesseract that
was inside it.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:13.320
He opens a portal, summoning a Lovecraftian
horror from beyond the veil of existence to

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.550
bring about Hydra's reign of terror.

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:19.210
The terrible beastie has more than a little
in common with the classic Marvel monster

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:20.610
Shuma-Gorath.

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:25.540
But as has often been said, he who lives by
the unspeakable alien abomination must also

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:28.350
die by the unspeakable alien abomination.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:32.860
And so the Red Skull gets smeared into a Red
Paste, hoisted by his own extradimensional

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:33.860
tentacle.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:38.080
Afterward, Stark does like a good sci-fi super
scientist is supposed to do and hits some

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:42.700
buttons frantically, trying to reverse the
polarity hard enough to make the rapidly-expanding

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:46.230
squid bits go back to where they came from,
but to no avail.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:50.510
Steve Rogers, now rescued and back in his
jury rigged, patch-job-powered armor, runs

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:53.380
out of juice before he can make much of an
impact.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:57.390
The only solution to the tentacle problem
is for Captain Carter to personally shove

00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:59.880
the eldritch horror back from whence it came.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.140
She disappears into the unknown, promising
Steve a dance on her way out.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:05.700
Everything fades to black.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:11.200
Almost 70 years later, Carter returns, flanked
by cuts of jumbo squid, out of a Cosmic Cube-induced

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:16.120
portal at what looks like the Joint Dark Energy
Mission Facility in the Mojave Desert from

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:17.300
2012's "Avengers."

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:21.660
A super soldier, plucked from history, stands
ripe with promise of becoming the hero of

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:22.660
a new era.

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:24.620
It's all terribly familiar.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:25.620
So what did we learn here today?

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:30.470
A lot is implied by the universe presented
in the first episode of "What If...?"

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:34.730
First off, Howard Stark was capable of coming
up with an Iron Man suit capable of harnessing

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:38.650
the power of the Tesseract, potentially shifting
the course of human civilization.

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:42.831
But the really intriguing question that the
episode leaves open is, "What would a world

00:03:42.831 --> 00:03:44.700
without Peggy Carter look like?"

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:48.530
We know that Agent Carter was instrumental
in the creation of S.H.I.E.L.D. during the

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:54.250
20th century of the MCU proper -- an organization
that itself became an unknowing incubation

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:55.680
chamber for Hydra.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:59.430
The Dark Energy lab seen at the end of the
episode looks less well-manned than the one

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:00.580
from "Avengers."

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:04.680
Maybe it's a coincidence, or maybe Hydra got
more of a foothold without Peggy around to

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:05.680
keep an eye on things.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:10.220
We don't see anything about the world outside
the lab circa 2012, but it's hard to imagine

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:15.030
that things went better without the supervision
of one of the Allied Forces' most legendary

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:18.480
agents, even with Bucky and Steve set up to
stand in for her.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:23.030
Without Bucky to use as their Vibranium-armed
super soldier, Hydra wouldn't have had their

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:30.260
go-to agent available to kill Howard and Maria
Stark on December 16, 1991.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:36.030
"Wow, thanks, you almost ripped my arm off."

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:39.780
Without his father's absence, Tony might not
have had any reason to rise to the occasion

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.710
and learn how to take the wheel of his old
man's company.

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:47.410
What's worse, with Iron Man-adjacent technology
entering into play some six decades early,

00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:50.360
a whole new set of potential travesties would
be lined up.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:51.360
Assuming that the U.S.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:55.771
Government retained the rights to the invention,
the course of manned warfare on Earth would

00:04:55.771 --> 00:05:00.500
have drastically changed, and an international
race to create powered suits would have likely

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:05.600
kicked off during the Cold War, opening the
door to mid-20th century versions of the Crimson

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.400
Dynamo, Titanium Man, and Iron Monger.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:12.890
Then again, maybe Howard Stark held onto the
technology for himself and went into business

00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:17.900
selling Iron Man suits, powered by Tesseract
energy, with the Cube no longer destined to

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:22.760
sit in mothballs for decades in the wake of
his up-close comprehension of its potential.

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:27.550
Maybe Howard used the Tesseract and its physics-defying
abilities to finally realize that new element

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:31.670
that he'd had his eye on but couldn't create
thanks to the limitations of his day.

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:35.610
Dozens of conflicts across U.S. history would
suddenly be shifted thanks to the presence

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:37.370
of legions of Iron Men.

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:42.180
Perhaps Howard refuses to relinquish the Tesseract
to Project P.E.G.A.S.U.S., halting the creation

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:46.130
of Captain Marvel and dooming the Earth upon
the arrival of Thanos.

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:50.360
The implications are countless… or maybe
we're just really overthinking a cartoon,

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:52.330
and everything ends up more or less the same.

